# AddAggregationManagementFeatureLabel

The full function body:

```go
func AddAggregationManagementFeatureLabel(obj metav1.Object) {
	labels := obj.GetLabels()
	if labels == nil {
		labels = map[string]string{}
	}
	labels[rbac.AggregationManagementFeatureLabel] = "true"
	obj.SetLabels(labels)
}
```

This function retrieves the object's labels, initializes an empty map if none exist, sets the aggregation management label to `"true"`, then writes the labels back to the object.

# handleAggregationMigration

**Signature** (a generic function over type `T`):

```go
func handleAggregationMigration[T any](
	resource T,
	labels map[string]string,
	updateLabels func(T, map[string]string) (T, error),
	deleteAggregatedResources func(T) error,
	deleteLegacyResources func(T) error,
) (T, error) {
```

**Body:** It first makes a copy of the labels map and checks whether the aggregation feature label is present by evaluating `labelCopy[rbac.AggregationFeatureLabel] == "true"`.

**Feature-flag-disabled case with label present:**

When the feature is off (`!features.AggregatedRoleTemplates.Enabled()`) and the label exists (`hasLabel`), the code executes:

```go
delete(labelCopy, rbac.AggregationFeatureLabel)
updated, err := updateLabels(resource, labelCopy)
if err != nil {
    return updated, err
}
return updated, deleteAggregatedResources(updated)
```

In plain terms: it removes the aggregation label from the copied map, persists the updated resource via `updateLabels`, and — assuming no error occurred — calls `deleteAggregatedResources` on the updated resource to clean up aggregation-created resources. If the flag is disabled but the label is already absent, it simply returns the resource unchanged (`return resource, nil`).

